speechtext:
I do not think it is necessary to take up much of the time of the House in arguing in favour of the amendment to the amendment, simply because it seems to me the consensus of opinion of the House at the present time is very nearly unanimous in favour of it. I desire to make one or two comments on the statements made by the two hon. gentlemen who spoke this afternoon, and particularly on the statement made by the hon.
[Mr. Manion.}
gentleman from Fort William (Mr. Manion). It seems to me that the hon. gentleman can scarcely define what the name of his party should be, or at least, he objects to the party being termed Tory. The thought occurs to me that we might term it the prehistoric party of Canada. That might apply to it all right. Of course, if that is not acceptable, they do not have to accept it.

Here is a resolution which was passed at the Imperial Conference of 1921:
Having regard to the constitutional development since 1917, no advantage is to be gained by holding a constitutional conference;
Mr. Lloyd George, in the course of his speech in the House of Commons on the 14th of December, 1921, referring to the dominions, spoke as follows-
